{
  "dom_id": "step-q344104",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/21e0ed364f3a04068c7f3775715432b218be2ada6b54d97ae6836f28528006da.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$\n\nGiven that $s(9)=-2,$ what is the maximum value ${s}_{\\text{max}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $s(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(9) & =-2 \\\\ s(0) & =s(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{0}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =s(9)-{\\int }_{0}^{9}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2-(-6) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,4\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$",
